        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "84044da6-947f-55dc-8c80-12464beb9a6a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Flanary Archeological Site, Dungannon, Scott County, Virginia, Clinch River\n- People: Thomas Walker\n- Events: Thomas Walker's expedition\n- Concepts: archaeological occupation periods, terminus ad quem\n\nFacts:\n- The Flanary Archeological Site is a historic archaeological site near Dungannon in Scott County, Virginia, United States, across the Clinch River from Dungannon.\n- The Flanary Archeological Site was inhabited as early as 6000 BC and remained in periodic use into the Woodland period, with possible occupation continuing until about AD 1600.\n- Thomas Walker was a Virginia physician and explorer who lived from January 25, 1715, to November 9, 1794.\n- In the mid-18th century, Thomas Walker took part in an expedition to the region beyond the Allegheny Mountains in the unsettled area of British North America.\n- The terminus ad quem for occupation at the Flanary Archeological Site is 1750, when Thomas Walker's expedition passed through the area and found no Indian villages.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b52979d5-89c2-50b1-b501-98186577693a",
        "text": "Page: Flanary Archeological Site. Sentence: Flanary Archeological Site is a historic archaeological site located near Dungannon in Scott County, Virginia, United States.\nPage: Flanary Archeological Site. Sentence: Located across the Clinch River from Dungannon, the site was inhabited as early as 6000 BC and remained in periodic use into the Woodland period, with occupation potentially continuing until c. AD 1600.\nPage: Flanary Archeological Site. Sentence: The terminus ad quem for occupation is 1750, when Thomas Walker's expedition passed through the area and found no Indian villages.\nPage: Thomas Walker (explorer). Sentence: Thomas Walker (January 25, 1715 – November 9, 1794) was a distinguished physician and explorer from Virginia; in the mid-18th century, he was part of an expedition to the region beyond the Allegheny Mountains and the unsettled area of British North America.",
